<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pilgrimage</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as pilgrim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A pilgrimage is a journey, often on foot, to a holy place of special significance to the followers of a religion, after which the pilgrim returns to daily life; the word pilgrim comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> peregrinus, one who has come from afar. The practice predates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>: the faiths of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> knew it, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> people consulted the oracles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona</a> and Delphi, and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> pilgrims travelled to Shiloh, Dan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> is a focus for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, Christianity and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, obligatory for Muslims who are able, draws two or three million people a year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrims gather at holy cities, rivers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbh-mela">Kumbh Mela</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A pilgrimage can lead to a personal transformation, after which the pilgrim returns to daily life. It is typically a physical journey to a shrine or another place of importance to a person's belief, though it can also be a metaphorical journey into one's own beliefs. Many religions attach importance to the places where founders or saints were born, died or were called, where miracles were performed, or where a deity is said to live. Devotees go to be healed, to have questions answered or to gain some other spiritual benefit, for themselves or on behalf of the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The practice is older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. It is found in the faiths of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in the Mithraic period, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> pilgrimages could be personal or state-sponsored, and the Eleusinian mysteries included a procession from the Athenian cemetery <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerameikos">Kerameikos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> along the Sacred Way. In early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> history pilgrims went to Shiloh, Dan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and the Three Pilgrimage Festivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, Shavuot and Sukkot required adult men to visit the Temple.</p><p>Surviving descriptions of Christian journeys to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> date from the 4th century, when church fathers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Saint Jerome</a> encouraged it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, established it. The early Celtic Christian Peregrinari Pro Christ left home for an unknown destination, and their wanderings often founded abbeys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the Middle Ages Christian pilgrimage became an economic sector, first through facilities at popular destinations and later through organised group trips around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>; pilgrims bought and wore badges as souvenirs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and the Way of St. James to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> were major goals. Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and elsewhere, many for the Hoshana Rabbah ceremony on the Mount of Olives.</p><p>Buddhists visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kusinara</a>, which are tied to the life of the Buddha. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> is one of the five pillars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, required once in a lifetime of adult Muslims who are able. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> does not count pilgrimage as an act of merit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pilgrimage” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
